    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d355">

                <pb facs="0503" n="389" sameAs="frus:frus1924v01/pg389"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/5707: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Chargé in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Mayer</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-11-05T18:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">November 5,
                                1924—6 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>269. Tokyo Embassy telegraphs November 5<anchor n="18" xml:id="d355-f1"/> that
                    Japanese Consul at Tsingtau advises his Government our Consul there has agreed
                    with Japanese and British colleagues to advise Wu Pei-fu not to land in the
                    event of his arrival there.</p>
                <pb facs="0504" n="390" xml:id="pg390"/>
                <p>Please advise Consul immediately that the Department could not approve such
                    intervention in Chinese internal affairs.</p>
                <p>Repeat to Tokyo as Department’s No. 180.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d355-f1" n="18" xml:id="d355-f1-content">
                <p>Telegram not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
